/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Mass transfer is the movement of a component inside a mixture, or from one phase to another, driven by a difference in concentration.
   - Compare the three transport subjects.
   - The subject matters because most separations in a chemical plant, apart from mechanical ones such as filtration, are built on this one movement.
   - A single phase cannot be separated by mass transfer alone.
   - Most books place drying with hot gas under phase creation, since heat makes the vapour; a few treat the hot gas as an added phase and put it under phase addition.
   - The same operations are also sorted by the phases that touch.
   - A third sorting is by contactor.
   - Five numbers describe composition, and people loosely call all of them concentration.
   - c is the total molar concentration of an ideal gas, mol/m³; P is the absolute pressure, Pa; T is the absolute temperature, K; R = 8.314 J/(mol·K) is the gas constant.
*/
