/* book exam traps: Writing 'concentration' without saying which one. Mole fraction and mass fraction are dimensionless and are not concentrations; a flux needs mol/m³ or kg/m³. Converting a molar concentration to a mass concentration multiplies by the molar mass in kg/mol. Alway | Using c = P/RT for a liquid. That formula holds for an ideal gas only. For a liquid use the molar density c = ρ/M, with ρ in kg/m³ and M in kg/mol. | Swapping y and x. By long convention y is the mole fraction in the vapour or gas and x is the mole fraction in the liquid. An answer that writes x for a gas stream loses marks even when the number is right. | Thinking mass transfer needs bulk flow. Diffusion carries a species through a completely still mixture; diffusion and bulk flow are separated properly in 1.2. | Classifying by equipment instead of by mechanism. A packed tower is not a separation; it is a contactor that can carry out absorption, stripping or distillation, and the examiner often asks you to say which. */
