/* book exam traps: Calling NA the diffusion flux. NA is the fixed-frame flux; JA is the diffusion flux. At large yA the bulk term yA(NA + NB) is a big part of NA. Setting NA = JA gives a wrong D in Chapter 1.3. | Assuming NA = −NB always. Only equimolar counter-diffusion has that; in general JA = −JB while NA + NB is not zero. | Mixing units of mass and molar flux. nA = MA NA; use kg/(m²·s) for nA and mol/(m²·s) for NA. | Using the molar average velocity u as the fluid flow velocity. u is a concentration-weighted mean of species velocities; the bulk term is yA(NA + NB), not cA times a guessed flow. | Treating eddy diffusivity DE as a fluid property. DE depends on turbulence and position and falls to zero at a wall; DAB depends on the pair, temperature and pressure. */
